1
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:23,500
She's primped. She's engaged. She's back.

2
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:41,000
Mulan II is a animation, comedy, family film directed by Lynne Southerland.

3
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:58,500
Fa Mulan gets the surprise of her young life when her love,
Captain Li Shang asks for her hand in marriage.

4
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:16,000
Before the two can have their happily ever after, the Emperor assigns them a
secret mission, to escort three princesses to Chang'an, China.

5
00:16:27,500 --> 00:16:33,500
Mushu is determined to drive a wedge between the couple after he learns that he
will lose his guardian job if Mulan marries into the Li family.

6
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:51,000
Mulan (voice), played by Ming-Na Wen.

7
00:23:02,500 --> 00:23:08,500
Shang (voice), played by BD Wong.

8
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:26,000
Mushu (voice), played by Mark Moseley.

9
00:29:37,500 --> 00:29:43,500
Mei (voice), played by Lucy Liu.

10
00:32:55,000 --> 00:33:01,000
Yao (voice), played by Harvey Fierstein.

11
00:36:12,500 --> 00:36:18,500
Ting Ting (voice), played by Sandra Oh.

12
00:39:30,000 --> 00:39:36,000
Mulan II is a 2004 American animated direct-to-video musical road adventure film produced by Disneytoon
Studios and distributed by Walt Disney Home Entertainment under the Walt Disney Pictures banner.

13
00:42:47,500 --> 00:42:53,500
The film was directed by Darrell Rooney and Lynne Southerland (in her directorial debut) and produced by
Jennifer Blohm, from a screenplay written by Michael Lucker, Chris Parker, and Roger S.

14
00:46:05,000 --> 00:46:11,000
Schulman, which was based on a story by Jenny Wingfield.

15
00:49:22,500 --> 00:49:28,500
It is the sequel to Walt Disney Feature Animation's 1998 animated feature film Mulan, featuring original songs that were written
by composer Jeanine Tesori and lyricist Alexa Junge and a musical score composed and conducted by Joel McNeely.

16
00:52:40,000 --> 00:52:46,000
Much of the voice cast from the first film returned, excluding Eddie Murphy (Mushu), Miriam Margolyes (The
Matchmaker), James Hong (Chi-Fu), Chris Sanders (Little Brother), and Matthew Wilder (Ling's singing voice).

17
00:55:57,500 --> 00:56:03,500
Murphy, Margolyes, and Sanders were replaced by Mark Moseley, April Winchell, and Frank
Welker, respectively, while Gedde Watanabe does his own singing for the sequel.

18
00:59:15,000 --> 00:59:21,000
Mulan II features Mulan and her new fiancé, General Li Shang, on a special mission: escorting the
Emperor's three daughters across the country to meet their soon-to-be fiancés.

19
01:02:32,500 --> 01:02:38,500
Meanwhile, Mushu attempts to break up their relationship to keep his pedestal.

20
01:05:50,000 --> 01:05:56,000
The film deals with arranged marriages, loyalty, relationships,
making choices, trust, and finding true love.

21
01:09:07,500 --> 01:09:13,500
The film was released as a direct-to-video title in the United States on
December 28, 2004 and received negative reviews from critics.

22
01:12:25,000 --> 01:12:31,000
The film explores themes of escape, arranged marriage, cartoon, musical, sequel.

23
01:15:42,500 --> 01:15:48,500
The tone is lighthearted.
